#--: Testing

This page says what tests the repository has, what each make target runs,
and how to add a test of each kind. Every gate needs only the compiler and a
C toolchain: the test runner (#~"src/ztestrunner.z"), the linter
(#~"src/zl.z") and the gate tool (#~"tools/zgate/") are zerolang programs.

#--: The tiers

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Target] [#th: What it runs] [#th: When]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"make check"] [#td: the parse-tier lint and the formatter check over every source file (#~"zl lint", #~"zl format --check"), and the complexity report] [#td: before every commit]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"make test"] [#td: builds the drivers and runs the test runner's default kinds] [#td: before every commit]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"make pre-push"] [#td: #~"check", #~"test" and #~"perf-strict"] [#td: before a push]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"make ci"] [#td: every gate (below)] [#td: at the end of a batch of work]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"make ci-windows"] [#td: the Windows legs on a real Windows host over ssh] [#td: when #~"make windows-due" says so]]

#~"make doc-check" runs the doccode and docpage kinds alone.
#~"make style-lint" adds the typecheck-tier lint (#~"zl lint --full") to
#~"make check"'s.

#---: make ci

#~"make ci" runs, in order:

#ol:
  [#*: #~"perf-strict", alone: the allocation ratchet (see
    [#> to=performance: Performance]). It runs first and alone because a
    #~"zc" running beside it in the repository root moves its count.]
  [#*: #~"ci-gates", in parallel: the legs #~"make ci-legs" lists. They are
    the per-batch gate's guards (#~"GATE_GUARDS": the lint, the warnings and
    #~"-Wpedantic" checks, the guards over the emitted C, the thread
    sanitizer, the math and Windows legs), the legs that rebuild the run
    corpus's C under another toolchain (#~"GATE_SAME_C": full UBSan,
    mingw-w64 and tcc for Windows under wine, MSVC under wine), and the legs
    only #~"make ci" runs (#~"CI_ONLY": the heavy corpus #~"ci-corpus", clang,
    #~"-O2", arm64 under qemu, the fuzzer's fixed seed, both build modes).
    A leg whose tools are missing says so and passes.]
  [#*: #~"test-bootstrap": the seed builds a compiler that rebuilds itself
    to a byte-identical fixpoint, under gcc, clang and the vendored tcc, and
    the three emit the same C.]
  [#*: #~"windows-due": one line saying when #~"make ci-windows" is due.]

#~"make ci-windows" runs the Windows legs on a real Windows machine and,
when they pass, writes #~"tests/windows-verified.txt" (#~"zgate
windows-record"), which the commit carries; #~"zgate windows-due" reads
it. #~"tools/windows/README.md" sets one up.

#--: The test runner

#~"out/ztestrunner --zc bin/zc --cc gcc --root ." runs the default kinds;
#~"--only KIND" runs one; #~"--heavy" adds the self-host ASan run, the
byte-identity fixpoint and the doc ratchet; #~"--jobs N" sets the job pool's
size; #~"ztestrunner --help" lists every flag. A command line it does not
accept is refused with exit 2. The kinds, as the runner's header lists them:

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Kind] [#th: What it checks]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"run"] [#td: each run case is emitted, compiled and run; its output and exit code match its goldens]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"error"] [#td: each error fixture fails with the #~"error[Exxxx]" lines of its golden]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"errcode"] [#td: every code #~"zast.errFacts" lists is reported by some golden]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"lone"] [#td: each run case, given one unrelated error, reports that error and no other]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"leak"] [#td: every buildable example and corpus program runs clean under its compiler's sanitizer]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"dump"] [#td: #~"zc dump --canon" of each dump case matches its golden]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"lsp"] [#td: framed JSON-RPC transcripts replayed through #~"zls" match their goldens]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"lexer", #~"parser", #~"program"] [#td: the token, tree and program goldens of every example]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"vfs", #~"smoke"] [#td: the file system's script cases, and each unit's no-argument harness]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"doccode"] [#td: every zerolang example in the documentation compiles, or fails as its #~"error=" says, and is laid out as #~"zl fmt" lays it]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"docpage"] [#td: each #~"docs/*.html" is what the renderer writes from its #~".pdoc", links name pages and anchors that exist, and the table of [#> to=./errors.html: Error Codes] is #~"zast.errFacts"']]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"usage"] [#td: each driver, given a command line, says what its case states and exits as it states]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"arch"] [#td: the architecture ratchet, #~"zl lint --arch" per rule and file]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"vocab"] [#td: the spec and the three highlighters carry the lexer's keywords and reserved words]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"pins"] [#td: the counts the compiler's own source is held at]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"fixtures"] [#td: every program with a #~"main" is in a case list, once, and no fixture breaks L023]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"natives", #~"usernative", #~"piece"] [#td: the native rows and their marks, a user unit's own natives, a record taken out of the emitter]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"statictcc", #~"cobj"] [#td: no libtcc symbol in the drivers, and a unit's C source built once through the object cache]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"emitted"] [#td: (only by #~"--only") what the emitted C of every program holds]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"probes"] [#td: (only by #~"--only") ThreadSanitizer reports a race made on purpose]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"pedantic"] [#td: (only by #~"--only") the C of the drivers and the run cases has no #~"-Wpedantic" diagnostic]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"ctest"] [#td: (only by #~"--only") the C test programs #~"tests/c/*_test.c" against their #~".out"]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"windows"] [#td: (only by #~"--only", on a Windows host, by #~"make windows-guest") what #~"make ci-windows" built, judged on Windows itself: the thread shim, the run corpus against the failure set wine sees, a console, drive paths, #~"zc build --cc cl" and #~"make ci"]]

#--: Adding a test

Each kind reads its cases from #~"tests/fixtures/":

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"A program that runs." A line #~"<name> <dir>" in
    #~"run_cases.txt" (#~"<dir>" is a list joined by #~":"; a line ending in
    #~"posix" is skipped for Windows), the program at #~"<dir>/<name>.z" --
    usually #~"tests/fixtures/emitc_corpus/" -- and its output in
    #~"run_golden/<name>.out". A #~"<name>.exit" holds an exit code other than
    0, and #~"<name>.<os>.out" the output for a system that prints its own.]
  [#*: #b:"A program the compiler refuses." #~"errors/<name>.z" and
    #~"errors/<name>.err", the #~"error[Exxxx]" lines and the count line. A
    #~"<name>.zcargs" gives the compiler flags (#~"--eager" for a unit whose
    error is in code nothing calls).]
  [#*: #b:"A dump." A line in #~"dump_cases.txt" and #~"dump_golden/<name>.canon".]
  [#*: #b:"A language-server session." #~"lsp_cases/<case>.msgs", one JSON
    message a line, and #~"lsp_golden/<case>.out"; #~"make regen-lsp-goldens"
    rewrites the goldens from a run.]
  [#*: #b:"A command line." A folder #~"usage/<case>/" with #~"args" (the
    driver's name and its arguments, where #~"$ROOT", #~"$SCRATCH" and
    #~"$HOST" stand for the repository, an empty folder of the case's own and
    the host's target), #~"expected.exit", and #~"expected.err" and
    #~"expected.out" for what the driver says. A #~"cwd" file holding
    #~"scratch" runs the driver in the scratch folder. #~"--regen-usage"
    rewrites the expected files.]
  [#*: #b:"An example in the documentation." A #~"#code language=zerolang"
    block in a #~".pdoc" page; see the style guide's "Examples in
    Documentation".]
  [#*: #b:"A C test." #~"tests/c/<name>_test.c" and #~"tests/c/<name>_test.out".]

#~"make regen-goldens" rewrites the lexer, parser and program goldens of the
examples. A golden that moves is a signal: read its diff before committing
it.

Lists of expected failures, each read by one leg: the data files of
#~"tests/known/" -- #~"leaks.zd", and one per toolchain (#~"tcc.zd",
#~"mingw.zd", ...) naming the cases it refuses or fails and the stage each
stops at -- and #~"fuzz.zd", the signatures the fixed-seed fuzz run is known
to show. The runner and the fuzzer read the data files with the standard
library's [#> to=./stdlib_zd.html: zd] unit.

#--: Ratchets

A ratchet holds a count at its committed value: a count above its row fails,
and a count below fails until the row is lowered in the same commit. A row
is raised only with the reason in the commit message.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"tests/fixtures/arch_baseline.txt" -- #~"zl lint --arch" findings
    per rule and file (the arch kind).]
  [#*: #~"tests/fixtures/doc_baseline.txt" -- undocumented definitions
    (L016), docs that only repeat their declaration (L024) and a body
    repeated across a file (L025), under #~"--heavy".]
  [#*: #~"tests/fixtures/doccode_baseline.txt" -- per page, the examples
    marked #~"check=none" and the checked ones #~"zl fmt" would lay out
    differently.]
  [#*: #~"ALLOC_BASELINE" in the Makefile -- the self-compile's heap
    allocations (#~"perf-strict").]
  [#*: #~"src/ztestrunner/expected.z" -- the pins and the emitted kind's
    baselines.]

#--: The per-batch gate

#~"bin/zgate" (#~"tools/zgate/", built by #~"make test") gates a batch of
commits against a snapshot of an earlier, gated state. Its home,
#~"$ZGATE_HOME", holds the snapshots and its lock.

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Command] [#th: What it does]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate snap TAG"] [#td: keeps #~"bin/zc", #~"bin/zl" and HEAD's tree as snapshot TAG; take it at a clean HEAD]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate pregate"] [#td: the quick checks of the #~".z" files changed since HEAD: format, lint, doc and arch counts]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate run TAG OUT"] [#td: the gate of HEAD against TAG, in a worktree of its own: check, test, clang, tcc, the C diff, the allocation split, perf-strict and the guards]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate cdiff REF NEW OUT"] [#td: the C (and dumps) of every program, the reference compiler's against the new one's]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate ab OLDZC NEWZC OLDTREE"] [#td: the allocation split of a self-compile: what the compiler's behaviour changed, and what its source did]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate asan ZC FILE"] [#td: builds FILE with ASan and UBSan and runs it]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate time ci|gates OUT"] [#td: #~"make ci", or each leg of #~"ci-gates" alone, timed]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"zgate perf report|strict|elision"] [#td: the measurements behind #~"make perf", #~"perf-strict" and #~"perf-elision"; the Makefile builds the binaries and keeps #~"ALLOC_BASELINE"]]

#--: Performance

Two binaries hold the same emitted C. #~"bin/zc" is the driver, built at
#~"-O2". #~"out/zc-perf" is the measured one, built by #~"PERFCC" at
#~"PERFOPT" (gcc #~"-O1") and rebuilt by every performance target: #~"-O2"
moves the wall time by about 30% and the allocations by nothing, so every
measurement is of #~"out/zc-perf". Never time #~"bin/zc" for a measurement.
Each target below builds what it measures and passes it to #~"zgate perf",
which measures and judges.

#ul:
  [#*: #~"make perf" -- the self-compile's line count, wall time (best of
    five) and peak memory, the parse / typecheck / emit split, and the
    allocation total when valgrind is installed.]
  [#*: #~"make perf-strict" -- the allocation ratchet: the heap blocks of
    one self-compile under valgrind, the same on every run, against
    #~"ALLOC_BASELINE". A count above it fails: lower the count, or raise the
    baseline with the reason in the commit. A count below it asks for the
    baseline to be lowered in the same commit. It refuses a clang-built
    binary, and it reports the instruction count beside the allocations.]
  [#*: #~"make perf-elision" -- the allocations an optimising C compiler
    deletes as write-only: #~"bin/zc.c" built twice by clang, once with
    #~"-fno-builtin-malloc". The difference belongs at zero.]
  [#*: #~"zgate ab" -- splits a change in allocations into the part the
    compiler's behaviour made (new compiler, old source) and the part its
    own source made (new source, new compiler).]

#b:"Judge speed by instructions, not cycles."
#~"perf stat -e instructions:u" counts work, and for one binary on one
input it is the same on every run, to a few hundredths of a percent, so a
difference of 0.2% can be read. Cycles count how long the core took to do
that work, and they move with whatever else runs: a thread on the same
physical core, other programs using the shared cache and memory, the
scheduler moving the process to another core, whether the kernel could give
the heap large pages, and where code and heap fall in memory. Three rounds
of the same binary on the same input have differed by 13% in cycles while
the instruction count held to 0.01%. A speed claim needs both binaries on
the same input: the self-compile cannot separate a slower compiler from a
larger source, so compare two compilers on one fixed program. Quote cycles
only when both binaries' spreads are tight, the rounds were interleaved and
the machine was quiet. Allocations count events, not time, and none of this
moves them.
